62 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511362 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511362 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511362 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511362 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "63236c73-41ba-5765-87b1-d084c15dd995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511370 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "76e1baa0-7895-58ed-a291-bc1c098ba28f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511385 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "921f7efe-43d0-57f9-9875-d7de999ae3c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511403 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a71ab316-18be-5962-b3fa-f8deb2262af0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511462 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+4)}{(x+4)(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne -4,1,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=1.$ So statement I is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x),$ and  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $1$ | $1.001$ | $1.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-99$ | $-999$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 1$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 1$ from the right. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement II is false and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3de7d0a26f6f171fc83bd885eed9ff9f50bddfc76b2f58e9477241fb874c9b7d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511498 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nSince $x=1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $0.9$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-4.74$ | $≈-49.75$ | $≈-499.75$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1dbd1e97af1e0fc6a7776fba28f6c4bcb081fa3213291f66aeefc8b3219331e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511515 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=1$ and $x=-5.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-5),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-5$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-5.1$ | $-5.01$ | $-5$ | $-4.99$ | $-4.9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-8.36$ | $-83.36$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $83.30$ | $8.30$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-5)$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-5)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and II is false.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-5$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41983c0d07e355a9bd12cf92af158c95f390a5785f4bbc1d08562c240f190623.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511570 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+3x+x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{x(x+3)+1(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(x+1)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nSince $x=-1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $-1.1$ | $-1.01$ | $-1.001$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-23.21$ | $≈-203.02$ | $≈-2\\,003.00$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2967a50cc89632da9292fc9192232f757ff3f3bec9304042ef6ec074882565b5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511581 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-2),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-2$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-2.01$ | $-2.001$ | $-2$ | $-1.999$ | $-1.99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈40.12$ | $≈400.12$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $≈-399.88$ | $≈-39.88$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-2)$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-2)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is false and II is true.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is also true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25e672e71c4268193c128687ba35eeca1e4bce8f0c355922a3b5df022740159c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511596 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511641 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511667 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511667